Software
OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect have different purposes.
OAuth 2.0 allows an application to get limited access to protected resources. 
OpenID Connect is an addition to, and enhancement of, OAuth 2.0 which introduces an identity layer to this protocol and facilitates applications to verify the identity of a user.
OpenID Connect can be used for user login in the national login service and OAuth 2.0 can be used for obtaining permission to access other resources.
Resource: OpenID Connect Core 1.0 - https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html

AI
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework can assist the authority in pre-managing risks when implementing the AI model to identify suspicious logins.
The authority should experiment on the suitability and validity of the model and look for any unfair results, privacy issues and security risks.
It should also check false alarms and cases where real attacks are missed.
After deployment, the model should be monitored and when necessary, people should review important decisions.
Resource: NIST AI Risk Management Framework FAQs - https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework/ai-risk-management-framework-faqs 

Mobile
Android restricts the access to what an app can do.
Certain permissions, like camera or location, need to be requested from the user during the application's runtime.
The app should provide a reason for the permission and should only request permissions when they are necessary to the functionality.
If user does not agree, the app should provide other features to operate when possible.
A government login app shouldn't request permissions that aren't related to its primary purpose.
Resource: Request runtime permissions - https://developer.android.com/training/permissions/requesting
